Z||000000||Groot||10-18-1999||03:04 PM||||Hi,<P>The capacitor of the Tach filter in my<BR>84-4cylinder is probably faulty.<BR>My tachometer is way too enthusiastic !<BR>Does someone know what kind of capacitor<BR>it should be and it's capacity ?<P>Thnx,<BR>Egbert<P>||||reg||3
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||10-18-1999||04:45 PM||||This (supposedly) is the schematic for the L4's tachfilter. This will not work on a V6!!!<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tachfilterL4.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||mwbackus||10-18-1999||06:53 PM||||Why not just replace the tach filter if it is defective?  They are no longer avail. from GM, but Rodney Dickman sells a replacement that should work fine:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/tachfilter.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/tachfilter.htm</A> <P>Mike||||reg||3
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||10-18-1999||07:46 PM||||Well, if you have a soldering iron you can build yourself the exact same tachfilter in about 2 minutes with $0.75 cents worth of parts. But if you want to build a really good tachfilter that lasts forever and will never cause any problems (and tachfilters can be the cause of a lot of problems), you need to spend a bit more time than that. There's a very good reason why the tachfilter needs to be in a solid metal (copper preferably) and grounded housing.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||10-18-1999||08:25 PM||||Well.. that makes sense. :-)  Perhaps more exploration into improvements on tach filter replacements would be in order.  I am always interested in novel ideas that reduce the repair costs of our cars.<P>Mike||||reg||7
